  "name": "Question 12",
  "problem": "Consider the equation ${v}^{2}-4a=16.$ For which values of $a$ does this equation have no real solutions?\n\n$a$\n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}",
  "worked_solution": "Let's try and solve using the square root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}^{2}-4a & =16 \\\\ {v}^{2} & =16+4a \\\\ \\sqrt{{v}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{16+4a} \\\\ |v| & =\\sqrt{16+4a}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation will have no real solutions if the expression under the square root is negative, which gives the following condition:\n\n$$\n16+4a<0\n$$\n\nWe solve this inequality for $a:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}16+4a & <0 \\\\ 4a & <-16 \\\\ a & <-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, any value of $a$ that is less than $-4$ will give an equation with no real solutions.",
